Z||000003||Old Lar||12-04-2000||04:30 PM||||Pontiac built 1252 T top 1988 Fieros. 91 on coupes,547 on Formulas and 614 on GTs.  Any others were aftermarket additions.  The RPO code for stock installations was CJB.  No 85 Fiero had a stock installed T top.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tom88gt||12-04-2000||06:01 PM||||Yes, '85 t-tops exist.  But only '88 were a "factory option".  All pre-'88s were aftermarket installs (done by the same company that did the '88 "factory" jobs).  I have a spare set of tops that I removed from a junkyard '85.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Jimmy||12-04-2000||10:27 PM||||Does anybody know how many Aftermarket T-TOP installations were done by C&C?  Or aproximately how many T-TOP kits were made?  TIA<P>Jim<BR>87 GT T-TOP<BR>87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000006||ndm86||12-05-2000||12:24 PM||||I worked at cars and concepts when they were doing the T-top's. To my reccolection only '88 but many dealers sent all years of Fieros to us and they were run down the same ass'y line as the 88's. I remember one car hauler had a load of yellow 88 gt's. What a sight! ||||reg||14
Z||000007||Tom88gt||12-05-2000||05:44 PM||||When did C&C start installing Fiero t-tops?  Was it just the 88 model year and any 84-87s were sent in for retrofitting?||||reg||5
Z||000008||mrfiero||12-05-2000||06:21 PM||||I believe they were doing conversions as far back as '85 or '86.  They were selling them in kit form and installing them too.  It took a few years before they could convince GM that their design was safe and didn't alter the structural integrity of the Fiero. GM finally allowed the T-Top conversion as a factory authorized/ordred option in 1988.  The cars were built then shipped to C&C for the T-Top installation.  These '88 Fieros have the RPO code  CJB to differentiate them from "aftermarket" install jobs.<P>The last C&C kit was installed by them in 1992 or 1993.....this was the last available kit that C&C owned.  I believe it went on a silver '88 GT.<P>As far as how many total kits were built.....we'll probably never knbow the exact number, but I would guesstimate 2000 or so aftermarket kits in addition to the 1252 factory jobs, so anywhere from 3000-3500 total T-Tops.  This is just my own estimate and is in no way set in stone.  I can't imagine much more than this number or we would see more T-Tops on the road.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||12-05-2000||06:42 PM||||If you worked at C&C maybe you can clear something for me. I get the impression from Fieroparts.com that the 88 parts are different from the other years. Do you know the story?||||reg||1
Z||000010||mrfiero||12-05-2000||07:11 PM||||I know this wasn't directed at me, but I think I can help.<P>All T-Tops for the Fiero (whether factory or aftermarket) are essentially the same.  However, there are some minor differences.<P>The main difference is in the main weatherseal.  The 'kits' have a separate rubber strip that holds/covers the headliner where it attaches to the T- roof.  The factory '88 T-Tops have this strip integrated into the weatherseal.  These are known as "ship-through", since '88s were shipped through C&C for the installation.<P>Another difference was the trim caps (A pillar, B pillar & maplight cover).   The factory '88 T-Tops had these trim pieces molded in the correct color, either beechwood or gray.  The trim pieces in the 'kits' were all molded light gray and painted to match the interior of the car being fitted.<P>Then there are differences in the weatherseal that attaches to the glass panel......the '88s used a different seal and aren't compatible with the 'kit' seals.<P><BR>Those are the main differences.....I'm sure someone else can fill us in on any others (if there are any). <p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 12-05-2000).]||||reg||1
